 "publicacion": {
  "titulo": "He called the 1929 crash and still went bankrupt",
  "descripcion": "Jesse Livermore built huge short positions in early 1929 and by spring was down over $6 million on paper. Later accounts say the crash netted him about $100 million. The public blamed him and he hired an armed bodyguard. In March 1934 he filed for bankruptcy with liabilities of $2,259,212.48, and his Great Neck estate had gone at auction. TIME's report that month never mentions a $100 million fortune, so treat that figure as legend. How he lost his money is still unknown. Short selling can lose more than the amount invested. Not financial advice. Sources: TIME, Mar 19, 1934; Wikipedia; New York Times, Oct 30, 1929.",
